The `ChiSquareTestImpl.chiSquare(double[], long[])` method calculates the chi-square test statistic based on the expected and observed values provided. The calculation involves iterating over the elements of the arrays and summing up the squared differences between observed and expected values normalized by the expected values.

The current bug report states that the method is returning an incorrect chi-squared value due to an implicit assumption that the sum of expected and observed values are equal. The specific issue lies in this part of the code:
```java
for (int i = 0; i < observed.length; i++) {
    dev = ((double) observed[i] - expected[i]);
    sumSq += dev * dev / expected[i];
}
```
The calculation is incorrect when the sum of the observed values does not equal the sum of the expected values. This can lead to an inaccurate result for the chi-square test statistic.

To diagnose this further, we need to verify whether the sum of the observed values is being considered correctly in the calculation.

